## Systematic Method Verification Process

1. First, list out ALL method calls in the file you're working with. Create a checklist.

2. For each method call, systematically:
   - Read the actual class/method definition from the source
   - Verify the constructor signature
   - Verify any class methods being called
   - Check if the method requires parameters that aren't obvious (e.g., from parent classes)
   - Document what you found (parameters, types, requirements)

3. Compare your findings against the current usage:
   - Mark which calls are correct
   - Mark which calls need fixes
   - Note any missing required parameters
   - Note any parameters that shouldn't be there

4. Only after verifying ALL method calls, make the necessary fixes:
   - Fix one method call at a time
   - Don't add "improvements" or "artistic changes"
   - Stick to fixing incorrect signatures
   - If you need to add a parameter, check if it's available in the current scope

Example of this process:
```python
# 1. List all methods
- LLMJSONService(quiet_mode=...)
- ScriptService.get_instance(llm)
- UniverseImporter().import_universe()
- Location.objects.get(name=...)
- Ship.create(name=..., cargo=...)
- Pilot.create(name=..., ship=...)

# 2. Verify each one
- Found: UniverseImporter needs xml_path in constructor
- Found: Ship.create needs location parameter
- Found: All other signatures match

# 3. Compare & Document
✓ LLMJSONService - correct
✓ ScriptService.get_instance - correct
✗ UniverseImporter - missing xml_path
✓ Location.objects.get - correct (Django ORM)
✗ Ship.create - missing location
✓ Pilot.create - correct

# 4. Fix systematically
- Add xml_path to UniverseImporter
- Add location to Ship.create
```

Remember:
- NEVER guess about method signatures
- NEVER add "improvements" while fixing signatures
- ALWAYS verify before changing
- If you can't verify a method, STOP and ask for help
- Keep a checklist of what you've verified
- Fix ONLY what's wrong, nothing more

When in doubt, it's better to stop and ask than to guess and introduce bugs. This systematic process helps prevent errors and maintains code quality.